  {
   "id": "zTNhX1zeBQAJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2015-10-20T23:00:00Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] How is Rstan's warmup works? Warming up takes a lot of time",
   "parts": [
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>&gt; On Oct 20, 2015, at 6:56 PM, Jiang Du &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jiang...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I guess there are a lot of things going on within the warming up iterations. If I set warmup=3, iteration=10, the whole process takes about 20 seconds. However, if I set warmup=30, iteration=130, the total time is not 20*10 seconds, and actually, the first 13 warmups took several minutes, and after a long time, the second 13 warmups is still running now. \r<br>\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "There&#39;s a description in the part of the manual about algorithms.  We\r<br>recommend running at least 100 warmup iterations.  Usually the first\r<br>few don&#39;t do much because the scale&#39;s off.  Then they hit increments where\r<br>step size is fit, and the result is often a perceived hang on Stan&#39;s part\r<br>because it moves to doing way more steps per iteration to get stability.\r<br>\r<br>Perhaps not surprisingly, it&#39;s very hard for us to read all these models\r<br>and figure out what they&#39;re going to do without spending lots of time\r<br>diving into the details.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>&gt; Thanks to Ben Goodrich&#39;s link to a paper implementing Kronecker product, my model seems having more hope to run, at least for the 10 iterations. However, it stuck again if I tried to have a longer iteration number with more warmups.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Is there a problem with my code, or it&#39;s just the model&#39;s problem, like, the number of parameters I have.\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; functions {\r<br>&gt; // return (A \\otimes B) v where:\r<br>&gt; // A is n1 x n1, B = n2 x n2, V = n2 x n1 = reshape(v,n2,n1)\r<br>&gt; matrix kron_mvprod(matrix A, matrix B, matrix V) {\r<br>&gt; return transpose(A * transpose(B * V));\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; data{\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower=1&gt; I;//counties\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower=1&gt; T;//time periods\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower=1&gt; K;//genders\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower=1&gt; J;//age groups\r<br>&gt;   \r<br>&gt;   matrix[I,I] Cmatrix;//adjacency matrix\r<br>&gt;   matrix[I,I] Identity;//Identity matrix\r<br>&gt;   real max_rho;//upper boundary of rho,to make precision matrix p.d.\r<br>&gt;   matrix[K,K] W;//prior of SIGMA\r<br>&gt;   vector[I] lambda;//eigens of Cmatrix\r<br>&gt;   vector[I*T*K] n[J];//obsed population\r<br>&gt;   //vector[I*T*K] y[J];//obsed data\r<br>&gt;   int&lt;lower=0&gt; y[J,I*T*K];\r<br>&gt;   matrix[T,T] A;//temporal smoothing matrix\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; transformed data{\r<br>&gt;   int KT;\r<br>&gt;   KT &lt;- K*T;\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; parameters{\r<br>&gt;   matrix[I,T*K] Z[J];\r<br>&gt;   matrix[I,T*K] V[J];\r<br>&gt;   matrix[K,K] SIGMA[J];\r<br>&gt;   real&lt;lower=0&gt; delta0;\r<br>&gt;   //real&lt;lower=0,upper=max_rho&gt; rho[J];\r<br>&gt;   real rho[J];\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; transformed parameters{\r<br>&gt;   matrix[I,I] B;\r<br>&gt;   matrix[K,K] sig_inv;\r<br>&gt;   matrix[K*T,K*T] first_product;\r<br>&gt;   real log_rho[J];\r<br>&gt;   vector[I*T*K] log_possion_mean[J];\r<br>&gt;   vector[I*T*K] vec_Z[J];\r<br>&gt;   vector[I*T*K] vec_V[J];\r<br>&gt;   //manually calculate the inverse of 2 by 2 matrix sig_inv as follows:\r<br>&gt;   real dinominator;\r<br>&gt;   for(j in 1:J){\r<br>&gt;     vec_Z[j] &lt;- to_vector(Z[j]);\r<br>&gt;     vec_V[j] &lt;- to_vector(V[j]);\r<br>&gt;     log_possion_mean[j] &lt;- log(n[j])+vec_V[j];\r<br>&gt;     B &lt;-  Identity - rho[j]*Cmatrix;\r<br>&gt;     dinominator &lt;- SIGMA[j][1,1]*SIGMA[j][1,1] - SIGMA[j][1,2]^2;\r<br>&gt;     \r<br>&gt;     sig_inv[1,1] &lt;- SIGMA[j][2,2]/dinominator;\r<br>&gt;     sig_inv[1,2] &lt;- -SIGMA[j][1,2]/dinominator;//negative sign, notice\r<br>&gt;     sig_inv[2,1] &lt;- sig_inv[1,2];\r<br>&gt;     sig_inv[2,2] &lt;- SIGMA[j][1,1]/dinominator;\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt;     //first product\r<br>&gt;     for(d1 in 1:K){\r<br>&gt;       for(d2 in 1:K){\r<br>&gt;         for(s1 in 1:T){\r<br>&gt;           for(s2 in 1:T){\r<br>&gt;             first_product[(d1-1)*T+s1, (d2-1)*T+s2]&lt;- sig_inv[d1,d2]*A[s1,s2];\r<br>&gt;           }\r<br>&gt;         }\r<br>&gt;         \r<br>&gt;       }\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;     log_rho[j] &lt;- 0.5*sum(1-rho[j]*lambda)*(T-2)*K-0.5*sum(Z[j].* kron_mvprod(first_product, B, Z[j]));\r<br>&gt;   }\r<br>&gt;   \r<br>&gt; } \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; model{\r<br>&gt;   delta0 ~ inv_gamma(2.0, 0.005);\r<br>&gt;   for(j in 1:J){\r<br>&gt;     rho[j] ~ uniform(0, max_rho);\r<br>&gt;     SIGMA[j] ~ inv_wishart(2,W);\r<br>&gt;     increment_log_prob(log_rho[j]);\r<br>&gt;     vec_V[j] ~ normal(vec_Z[j], delta0);//this can be improved with std normal then transforming\r<br>&gt;     y[j] ~ poisson_log(log_possion_mean[j]);\r<br>&gt;   }\r<br>&gt; }\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Well it has a warning, but I suppose I can neglect it. I&#39;m just doing a reshaping for the matrix to vector.\r<br>&gt; &gt; fit &lt;- stan(file = &#39;FullModel.stan&#39;, data = CRC_data, \r<br>&gt; +             iter = 130, warmup=30, chains = 1, init=list(init_data))\r<br>&gt; DIAGNOSTIC(S) FROM PARSER:\r<br>&gt; Warning (non-fatal):\r<br>&gt; Left-hand side of sampling statement (~) may contain a non-linear transform of a parameter or local variable.\r<br>&gt; If so, you need to call increment_log_prob() with the log absolute determinant of the Jacobian of the transform.\r<br>&gt; Left-hand-side of sampling statement:\r<br>&gt;     vec_V[j] ~ normal(...)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; SAMPLING FOR MODEL &#39;FullModel&#39; NOW (CHAIN 1).\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Chain 1, Iteration:   1 / 130 [  0%]  (Warmup)\r<br>&gt; Chain 1, Iteration:  13 / 130 [ 10%]  (Warmup) \r<br>&gt; And it stopped here. \r<br>&gt; \r<br>"
    ],
